<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-vector-equality">
<title>Vector equality</title>

<introduction>

	<p>
	In each case,
	determine the value(s) of <m> k </m>,
	if any,
	for which the vector equality is true.
	</p>

</introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> (k + 2, k^2, 1, k + 6) = (-3, 25, 1, 1) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><m> k = -5 </m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> (k + 2, k^2, 1, k + 6) = (-3, 25, -1, 1) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		There is no such value of <m> k </m>,
		as the two vectors will always disagree in the third component.
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> (k + 2, k^2, 1, k + 6, 0) = (-3, 25, 1, 1) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		There is no such value of <m> k </m>,
		as the first vector is <m>5</m>-dimensional while the second vector is only <m>4</m>-dimensional.
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
